\section{Taylor Polynomials}

\lessonobjective{construct a Taylor polynomial from derivative data and explain derivative matching at the center.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={A polynomial can be forced to imitate local behavior}]

A tangent line matches a function value and first derivative at one point. A quadratic Taylor polynomial also matches the second derivative. A degree-\(n\) Taylor polynomial matches every derivative through order \(n\) at the center. The coefficients are therefore determined, not guessed.



Derivative matching explains why Taylor polynomials are locally accurate. Near the center, the first unmatched derivative controls the leading error. Increasing degree does not automatically guarantee good behavior far away, but it incorporates more local information and often enlarges the useful interval.

\end{bgexposition}

\begin{bgbridge}[title={Match local derivative data one layer at a time}]
A Taylor polynomial is built to share a function's value, slope, curvature, and higher derivatives at one center. The constant term matches the value; the linear term matches the slope; the quadratic term repairs curvature; each additional degree matches one more derivative.

The factorial denominator is not an arbitrary convention. Differentiating \((x-a)^n\) exactly \(n\) times produces \(n!\), so dividing by \(n!\) makes the coefficient reproduce \(f^{(n)}(a)\). The polynomial is a local model whose useful range depends on the remainder, not merely on its degree.
\end{bgbridge}

\begin{bgvisual}{u4b-taylor-degree-ladder}{Higher Taylor degree matches more local shape}{A function near the center is compared with constant, tangent-line, quadratic, and cubic Taylor approximations. Each added degree follows the function farther from the center.}
