\section{The Binomial Series}

\lessonobjective{use the generalized binomial expansion for noninteger powers and determine its convergence interval.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={The finite binomial theorem has an infinite extension}]

For a nonnegative integer exponent, \((1+x)^m\) is a finite polynomial. For an arbitrary real exponent \(\alpha\), the same coefficient pattern continues indefinitely using generalized binomial coefficients. The resulting power series represents \((1+x)^\alpha\) for \(|x|<1\).



The expansion unifies many useful approximations, including square roots and reciprocal powers. Coefficients are best generated recursively rather than memorized. Endpoint behavior depends on \(\alpha\) and requires separate analysis, so the standard statement emphasizes the open interval.

\end{bgexposition}

\begin{bgbridge}[title={The binomial theorem continues beyond integer powers}]
For a nonnegative integer exponent, the binomial expansion terminates. For a general real exponent \(\alpha\), the same coefficient pattern continues indefinitely and produces a power series for \((1+x)^\alpha\) when \(|x|<1\).

The generalized coefficients
\[
\binom{\alpha}{n}=\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-n+1)}{n!}
\]
encode the changing numerator factors. When \(\alpha\) is a nonnegative integer, one factor eventually becomes zero and the familiar finite theorem is recovered.
\end{bgbridge}

